.TH PIPE 2
.SH NAME
pipe \- create an interprocess channel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "pipe :: proc() -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pipe
creates a pipe: two descriptors, each the read end of a byte ring the
other end writes.
Data written to one end is read back from the other, in order, with no
message boundary preserved.
A read on an empty ring parks until the other end writes or is closed; a
write on a full ring parks until the other end reads.
.PP
The answer packs both descriptors into one number, end 0 in the low byte
and end 1 in the next; a caller separates them with
.B abi.pipe_ends
or the equivalent shift and mask.
Each end is an ordinary descriptor from there on: it travels to a child
through
.IR spawn ,
closes through
.IR close ,
and may be posted under
.B /srv
to make the far side of the pipe a service (see
.IR bind (2)
and
.IR srv (3)).
.PP
When the writing end is closed or its process ends, a read on the other
end drains what remains buffered and then answers zero, end of file.
When the reading end is closed or its process ends, a write on the other
end fails.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B sys/abi/abi.odin
.br
.B kernel/pipe/pipe.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR read (2),
.IR pipe (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers the packed pair of descriptors on success and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
.B ENOSPC
is a machine already holding as many pipes as it may.
.SH BUGS
A pipe holds 2048 bytes in each direction, and the machine holds at most
eight pipes at once.
If a read or a write of a pipe is cut short by a note, some unknown
number of bytes may have already been transferred.
